==Campaign==
 
According to political analysts Salla Laaksonen and Kimmo Elo, the main dividing line between the presidential candidates in this election is not the traditional division over ideology, but instead voters are placing more stress on a candidate's views on the EU and social issues.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/presidentti_valitaan_uusin_arvoin_3184874.html |title=Presidentti valitaan uusin arvoin {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 }}{{Dead link|date=August 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

Professor Leif Åberg has described the campaign as "relatively civil,"<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/presidenttiehdokkaiden_kampittamista_yritetaan_kaikin_keinoin_3176101.html |title=Presidenttiehdokkaiden kampittamista yritetään kaikin keinoin {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 |archive-date=18 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120118062656/http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/presidenttiehdokkaiden_kampittamista_yritetaan_kaikin_keinoin_3176101.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> but there have been a few accusations of negative campaigning. Some of Pekka Haavisto's supporters felt offended over a television advert by the Paavo Väyrynen campaign which says that "a house needs a master and a mistress." Haavisto's supporters interpreted this as a taunt on Haavisto's relationship status (Haavisto lives in a registered partnership with another man). Väyrynen responded by rejecting this interpretation and in turn accused Haavisto's supporters of deliberately twisting his words.<ref>{{cite web|author=Elisa Rimaila |url=http://www.iltasanomat.fi/presidentinvaalit/vayrynen-syyttaa-haaviston-kannattajia-mustamaalaamisesta/art-1288443392615.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120120213601/http://www.iltasanomat.fi/presidentinvaalit/vayrynen-syyttaa-haaviston-kannattajia-mustamaalaamisesta/art-1288443392615.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 January 2012 |title=Väyrynen syyttää Haaviston kannattajia mustamaalaamisesta – Ilta-Sanomat |publisher=Iltasanomat.fi |date=19 January 2012 |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> The satirical television programme ''YleLeaks'' mocked the election in mid-December 2011. Niinistö's wife, Jenni Haukio, complained with a letter to ''YLE'''s ''A Studio'' saying that she was a private person. An ''A-Studio'' political reporter also criticised ''[[Iltalehti]]'' of news in favour of Sauli Niinistö.<ref>[http://www.yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2011/12/vaarin_naurettu_3099652.html Väärin naurettu] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120220081931/http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2011/12/vaarin_naurettu_3099652.html |date=20 February 2012 }} yle 13 December 2011</ref> Foreign Minister [[Erkki Tuomioja]] accused editor A.-P. Pietilä of undervaluing Haavisto's abilities in foreign policy based on his companion; Pietilä wrote, citing an unnamed civil servant, that Haavisto's election as president might complicate Finland's bid for being elected as a non-permanent member in the [[UN Security Council]] as several countries where homosexuality is outlawed might oppose the bid in this scenario. (Pietilä works for ''[[Suomen Lehtiyhtymä]]'', which is owned by the NCP MP [[Eero Lehti]]).<ref>[http://www.yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/tuomioja_syyttaa_paatoimittaja_pietilaa_loanheitosta_3205364.html Tuomioja syyttää päätoimittaja Pietilää loanheitosta ] yle 26 January 2012</ref>

===Funding===
The parties budgeted less money for their campaigns than during the last presidential election in [[2006 Finnish presidential election|2006]]. This was in part due to the [[2008 financial crisis]] and in part due to scandals involving campaign funding over the last few years. According to initial estimates, the four largest parties planned to spend less than 3 million euros combined.<ref>{{cite web|author=Marjo Ollikainen |url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/Vaalirahakohu+romahdutti+puolueiden+kampanjakassat/a1305551658504 |title=Vaalirahakohu romahdutti puolueiden kampanjakassat – Presidentinvaalit – Politiikka |publisher=HS.fi |access-date=13 January 2012}}</ref>

Of the second round candidates, Niinistö's budget was 1.2 million euros and Haavisto's budget was 725,000 euros.<ref>{{cite web|author=${when} |url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/Niinist%C3%B6+ker%C3%A4si+toisella+kierroksella+yli+170000+euroa/a1305554918967 |title=Niinistö keräsi toisella kierroksella yli 170 000 euroa – Sauli Niinistö – Politiikka |publisher=HS.fi |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nelonen.fi/uutiset/kotimaa/uutinen/haavisto-sai-puoli-miljoonaa-euroa-lis%C3%A4%C3%A4-vaalirahaa |title=Nelosen uutiset – Uutinen – Haavisto sai puoli miljoonaa euroa lisää vaalirahaa |publisher=Nelonen.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 |archive-date=4 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304002509/http://www.nelonen.fi/uutiset/kotimaa/uutinen/haavisto-sai-puoli-miljoonaa-euroa-lis%C3%A4%C3%A4-vaalirahaa |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Debates===
[[File:Valaffischer presidentvalet 2012 Gertrudsbacken.jpg|thumb|2012 Finnish presidential election posters, first round.]]
;First round
[[MTV3]] organised a debate with all eight candidates on 14 December. When discussing the [[European sovereign debt crisis]] six of the candidates believed in the survival of the euro, while Soini and Väyrynen believed that the [[eurozone]] would dissolve in one way or another.<ref>{{cite web|author=Nuutti |url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/MTV3n+presidenttitentti+Vain+Soini+ja+V%C3%A4yrynen+uskovat+euron+hajoavan/a1305551391828 |title=MTV3:n presidenttitentti: Vain Soini ja Väyrynen uskovat euron hajoavan – Presidentinvaalit – Politiikka |publisher=HS.fi |access-date=13 January 2012}}</ref> Väyrynen accused Lipponen and Niinistö of misleading the parliament, when Finland joined the common currency in the early years of the 2000s (when Lipponen and Niinistö were the leading figures of the government).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv3.fi/uutiset/presidentinvaalit2012/index.shtml/arkistot/kotimaa/2011/12/1460454/lipponen-vayryselle-ja-niinistolle-selvittakaa-eurovalinne-takahuoneessa |title=Lipponen Väyryselle ja Niinistölle: Selvittäkää eurovälinne takahuoneessa – MTV3.fi – Uutiset – Taustat – Presidentinvaalit 2012 |publisher=MTV3.fi |date=14 December 2011 |access-date=13 January 2012}}</ref> Soini was the only candidate to answer that as president he would address the issue of [[Karelian question in Finnish politics|returning Finnish Karelia]] to Finland in discussions with the Russian government.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.mtv3.fi/uutiset/presidentinvaalit2012/index.shtml/arkistot/kotimaa/2011/12/1460460/soini-keskustelisi-karjalan-palauttamisesta-venajan-kanssa |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130106193031/http://www.mtv3.fi/uutiset/presidentinvaalit2012/index.shtml/arkistot/kotimaa/2011/12/1460460/soini-keskustelisi-karjalan-palauttamisesta-venajan-kanssa |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 January 2013 |title=Soini keskustelisi Karjalan palauttamisesta Venäjän kanssa – MTV3.fi – Uutiset – Taustat – Presidentinvaalit 2012 |publisher=MTV3.fi |date=14 December 2011 |access-date=13 January 2012 }}</ref> All candidates were opposed to Finland joining [[NATO]], although for slightly differing reasons. Soini and Essayah underlined the importance of credible national defence, expressing concerns about defence cuts. Lipponen too was concerned about NATO membership's effects on the Defence Forces. Niinistö said that increasing security cooperation within the EU might make joining the NATO unnecessary in the long run. Biaudet stressed European defence as well, while Arhinmäki wanted to strengthen the role of the UN.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv3.fi/uutiset/presidentinvaalit2012/index.shtml/arkistot/kotimaa/2011/12/1460459/essayah-uskottava-puolustus-kaiken-kulmakivi |title=Essayah: Uskottava puolustus kaiken kulmakivi – MTV3.fi – Uutiset – Taustat – Presidentinvaalit 2012 |publisher=MTV3.fi |date=14 December 2011 |access-date=13 January 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv3.fi/uutiset/kotimaa.shtml/2011/12/1459906/soini-venaja-on-suurvaltana-potentiaalinen-uhka |title=Soini: Venäjä on suurvaltana potentiaalinen uhka – MTV3.fi – Uutiset – Kotimaa |publisher=MTV3.fi |date=14 December 2011 |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> EU issues were topmost in the [[YLE]] debate on 15 December as well.<ref>{{cite web|author=Nuutti |url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/Kannatusluvut+puhuttivat+presidenttiehdokkaita+Ylen+tentiss%C3%A4/a1305551476904 |title=Kannatusluvut puhuttivat presidenttiehdokkaita Ylen tentissä – Presidentinvaalit – Politiikka |publisher=HS.fi |access-date=13 January 2012}}</ref>

In a debate organised by [[Helsingin Sanomat]] and [[Nelonen (television)|Nelonen]] on 17 January, the last day of advance voting, all candidates expressed concerns regarding the social marginalisation of the youth. All eight were also critical toward possible increases of Finland's guarantees in the [[European Financial Stability Facility]], although Lipponen stressed the importance of European cooperation and Biaudet said that Finland should actively participate in the solving of the EU's debt problems. While seven candidates thought that the president's role in EU affairs is primarily supporting the cabinet led by the prime minister, Soini wanted to pursue more independent policies if elected. Niinistö, Haavisto, Arhinmäki and Biaudet were worried about the opinion polls' effect on tactical voting and were willing to ban the publishment of opinion polls two weeks prior to an election.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/ehdokkaat_puolustivat_nuorisoa_hsn_ja_nelosen_vaalitentissa_3183084.html?origin=rss |title=Ehdokkaat puolustivat nuorisoa HS:n ja Nelosen vaalitentissä {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 }}{{Dead link|date=August 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>

In a second MTV3 debate on 18 January Lipponen accused Niinistö of hypocrisy, saying that although Niinistö has spoken against greediness he has also accepted funding from millionaires, who, according to Lipponen, have laid off thousands of workers. Arhinmäki attacked Niinistö over Niinistö's condemnation of illegal strikes, with Arhinmäki himself maintaining that there is no such a thing as an illegal strike. Niinistö defended himself against his critics, saying that everyone has to participate in common efforts to thwart the [[2008 financial crisis]] and that labour disputes should be resolved within legal means.<ref>{{cite web|author=Teppo Ovaskainen |url=http://www.uusisuomi.fi/kotimaa/119474-lipponen-hyokkasi-niinisto-ahneus-ja-miljonaarit |title=Lipponen hyökkäsi: Niinistö, ahneus ja miljonäärit – Uusi Suomi |publisher=Uusisuomi.fi |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> Soini confronted Haavisto, saying that Haavisto's party, the Green League, wants to make people pay more for energy. Haavisto responded by slightly distancing himself from his party, saying that the election is about individual candidates instead of parties.<ref>{{cite web|author=IS |url=http://www.iltasanomat.fi/presidentinvaalit/soinilta-tayslaidallinen-haaviston-niskaan-kiitos-pitkasta-syntilistasta/art-1288443120039.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120120014525/http://www.iltasanomat.fi/presidentinvaalit/soinilta-tayslaidallinen-haaviston-niskaan-kiitos-pitkasta-syntilistasta/art-1288443120039.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=20 January 2012 |title=Soinilta täyslaidallinen Haaviston niskaan: "Kiitos pitkästä syntilistasta" – Ilta-Sanomat |publisher=Iltasanomat.fi |date=18 January 2012 |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref>

The final debate prior to the first round was held by YLE on 19 January.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/vaalivaittelyjen_loppuhuipennus_torstaina_3182518.html?origin=rss |title=Vaaliväittelyjen loppuhuipennus alkoi {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 }}{{Dead link|date=August 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> The candidates were asked to comment on former president [[Mauno Koivisto]]'s statement, where Koivisto expressed concern over the continued curtailment of the president's formal power. Soini and Väyrynen shared Koivisto's concern and were willing to increase the president's powers, while the other six candidates were content with the current situation.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/soini_ja_vayrynen_kaipaavat_vahvaa_presidenttia_3190077.html |title=Soini ja Väyrynen kaipaavat vahvaa presidenttiä {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 |archive-date=19 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120219032504/http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/soini_ja_vayrynen_kaipaavat_vahvaa_presidenttia_3190077.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> When discussing NATO, Väyrynen and Arhinmäki accused Niinistö and Lipponen of being too favourable toward NATO membership. Niinistö and Lipponen denied these charges, with the latter dismissing them as "conspiracy theories".<ref>{{cite web |url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/niinistoa_piikiteltiin_natosta_3190143.html |title=Niinistöä piikiteltiin Natosta {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012 |archive-date=19 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120219031911/http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/01/niinistoa_piikiteltiin_natosta_3190143.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[File:Tasavallanpresidentinvaihdos2012Halonen&Niinistö+Puheloinen.JPG|thumb|[[Sauli Niinistö]] and [[Tarja Halonen]]]]
;Second round
Helsingin Sanomat wrote that during a YLE debate on 30 January "practically the only question" to bring out differences between Niinistö and Haavisto was their stance on Finland's possible future membership of the UN Security Council. Niinistö said that, if a member, more active participation in [[peace keeping]] operations would be expected from Finland, while Haavisto emphasised civilian crisis management.<ref>{{cite web|author=${when} |url=http://www.hs.fi/politiikka/Ylen+tentti+Ehdokkaat+erimielisi%C3%A4+turvaneuvoston+haasteista+/a1305554575128 |title=Ylen tentti: Ehdokkaat erimielisiä turvaneuvoston haasteista – Presidentinvaalit – Politiikka |publisher=HS.fi |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> In another YLE debate on 2 February, economic issues showed some distinctions between the two candidates with Niinistö underlining the importance of economic growth and restraining the growth of debt and Haavisto expressing concerns about the impacts of economic growth on the environment and inequality.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/02/haavisto_ja_niinisto_nokittelivat_talousasioista_3227856.html |title=Haavisto ja Niinistö nokittelivat talousasioista {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref> Analysts Juhana Torkki and Pertti Timonen stated that both Niinistö and Haavisto tread carefully during campaigning prior to the runoff and that this campaign had less "drama" than that of the first round.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/uutiset/teemat/presidentinvaalit/2012/02/vaalien_suurin_draama_nahtiin_jo_ensimmaisella_kierroksella_3228272.html |title=Vaalien suurin draama nähtiin jo ensimmäisellä kierroksella {{pipe}} Presidentinvaalit {{pipe}} Teemat {{pipe}} YLE Uutiset |publisher=yle.fi |access-date=7 February 2012}}</ref>

